[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 7"""
 date="2026-11-13T11:48:07Z"
 content="""
The situation with proxied remotes is:

* --proxyconnections is the number of *idle* connections to cache.
  It is not a cap on the maximum number. If two requests come in to two
  different proxied remotes, a connection will be started to both,
  and once both are done, one of them will remain cached.
* --proxyconnections is per-repository, so even if it were an upper bound,
  there could be a lot of processes for proxied external special remotes
* Evicting a proxied connection from the pool doesn't stop the external
  special remote process. This is because Remote.list is still populated,
  and also Remote.External registers a cleanup action that stops it --
  which is another reference and keeps it running until quiesce.
  (I have a proof of concept fix for this in `p2phttpchill`.)

I think that --proxyconnections can stay as an idle connections cache
limit rather than an upper bound on the number of active connections.
But make it be across all served repos, rather than per-repo.

Add a cap on the number of total proxy connections. It could be the same as
the number of jobs.

It may make sense to have a separate AnnexWorkerPool that is used only for
proxy connections, and where each Annex state is used for a single
proxied connection. That way, when a proxied connection is evicted from
the pool, quiesce can be used to stop only it, and not other proxied
connections.
"""]]
